  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days..\n\nAnother morning of patchy fog is forecast across portions of the \nCWA. The highest chance of seeing visibility reductions will be in\nlow lying areas and river valleys near Miles City and Forsyth. \n\nAn area of weak ridging will remain over the Northern Rockies for\nthe next few days, before an even stronger ridge builds in late \nweekend. This will keep skies mostly clear allowing for good \ndiurnal swings, including highs above average. The normal high for\nthis time of year in most of our population centers is around \n50F, however highs in the upper 50s to low 60s will be commonplace\nfor the next week. As the high amplitude ridge builds in Sunday, \nsome locations may even have a chance to touch the 70s and record \nhighs!\n\nThe most impactful weather to speak about is the potential for \nsome breezy conditions in the western parts of our CWA this \nweekend. Two weak shortwaves will push through Montana (closer to \nthe MT/AB border) Saturday then Sunday. These vort maxes will come\nwith mainly dry conditions. \n\nThe first wave that moves through Saturday will be much more \nimpactful for areas to our NW but we will still see breezy winds. \nThe RAP is showing 700mb winds of roughly 30kts across central MT \nmidday which is roughly in line with what the long range models \nhave been showing for the past few runs. The wind direction looks \nto be slightly north of west which could limit some of the gap \ninfluence along the Big Timber to Harlowton corridor but not all \nof it. Wind gusts in this area could reach 40 mph during the day \nSaturday with even lower values for Billings and points east. \n\nThe second wave will move into NW MT during the day Sunday. As of\nthe latest model runs, it looks like this wave will bring \nstronger winds aloft, however it will be just a touch farther \nnorth than the Saturday wave. Stronger winds from the Big Timber \nto Harlowton region are anticipated though for two reasons. The \nfirst being that the winds aloft will be a little higher (around \n40kts according to the GFS) and the wind direction will be due \nwesterly. This could be conducive for gusts in the 50s mph along \nUS-191 Sunday afternoon. The rest of the region could see wind \ngusts in the 30s mph for much of the day. This will be something \nto keep an eye on though as if this wave does push a little more \nsouth or east then we could be talking about a more widespread \nevent with stronger winds. \n\nThe Livingston gap winds also look to occur this weekend. The \nGFS/GEFS are both showing pressure gradient values fron IDA- LWT \nof 10-14mb before sunrise both Saturday and Sunday morning. The \ninversion both nights does appear to setup pretty well, although \nany increase in high clouds from what is currently forecast could \nalter the inversion strength considerably. And, as mentioned \nabove, the 700mb winds wil be stronger Sunday AM. Other than that \nlast detail, most of the ingredients look fairly similar both \nmornings. Wind gusts in the 50s both mornings around Livingston \nshould be anticipated with a slightly higher likelihood on Sunday \nmorning.\n\nLooking ahead to next week, it appears that the overall pattern \nfor our region remains relatively quiet. Long range models do show\nmore weak short waves pass near the our CWA but none of which \nseem to be more impactful than some breezy winds or a few showers.\nLooking out towards the middle of the month though, both the GEFS\nand ECMWF Ensembles show a more progressive pattern with temps \ncloser to average. WMR",
